# Draft Development Concept Plan / Environmental Assessment: Gettysburg Little Round Top / Devil's Den, Gettysburg National Military Park (National Park Service, May 1985)
# A planning document of 1985: its 'Historic Scene' section (PDF p. 14, printed p. 9) states what the park believed of the south end of the second-day battlefield in 1863 and what remained in 1985.
# The text layer is a scan with letter spacing noise (G ettysb u rg); every quote is in the cleaned form (letters and digits match, spacing ignored); the page was looked at as an image and its printed page number (9) and the fraction in '15 1/2 miles' (read by the text layer as '151 ^') were read from the image.
@ NPS-DCP-LRT
%images 14

14/9 | fences | In 1863 about 15½ miles of historic fencing in the south end of the park, of six types | - | 1863-07 (statement of 1985 about the south end of the second-day battlefield) | stated | The plan says that in 1863 approximately 15½ miles of historic fencing existed in the south end of the park, of six types: stone, stone with a wood rider on top, post-and-rail, Virginia worm, post-and-board and picket | =In 1863 approximately {{151 ^|15½}} miles of historic fencing existed in the south end of the park. They were of six different types: stone, stone with a wood rider on top, post-and-rail, Virginia worm, post-and-board, and picket.
14/9 | fences | About 6 miles of the historic fencing remained in 1985 | - | ~1985 (condition of 1985 compared with the 15½ miles of 1863) | stated | The plan says that of the historic fencing of the south end approximately 6 miles remain at the time of the plan | =Today approximately 6 miles of historic fencing remain. | =In 1863 approximately {{151 ^|15½}} miles of historic fencing existed in the south end of the park.
14/9 | general | The farms, fences, roads, woods and rock-strewn lands of the south end are to a great extent as in 1863, except privately owned parcels and nonhistoric vegetation on lands clear in 1863 | - | ~1985 (condition of 1985 compared with the battle) | stated | The plan says the farms, fences, roads, woods and rock-strewn lands in the south end of the park are to a great extent as they were at the time of the battle, except that there are several privately owned parcels and extensive nonhistoric vegetation covers lands that were clear in 1863 | =The farms, fences, roads, woods, and rock-strewn lands in the south end of the park are to a great extent as they were at the time of the battle, except that there are several privately owned parcels and extensive nonhistoric vegetation covers lands that were clear in 1863.
14/9 | buildings | Seven historic farms on this part of the second-day battlefield: Rose, Sherfy, Bushman, Slyder, Snyder, G. W. Weikert, Trostle; three essentially as in 1863 | bld-rose-farm,bld-sherfy-farm,bld-slyder-farm,bld-snyder-farm,bld-weikert-farm,bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 (the farms named; the condition of three is as of 1985) | stated | The plan names seven historic farms on this part of the second-day battlefield, the Rose, Sherfy, Bushman, Slyder, Snyder, G. W. Weikert and Trostle farms, and says three of them are essentially as they were in 1863 without naming which | =On this part of the second-day battlefield are seven historic farms: the Rose, Sherfy, Bushman, Slyder, Snyder, G.W. Weikert, and Trostle farms. Three are essentially as they were in 1863.
14/9 | woodlots | By 1863 most woodlands had been cleared for farmland or pasture | - | 1863 | stated | The plan says that by 1863 most woodlands had been cleared for farmland or pasture, which is why the Round Top woods were important | =These areas were important because by 1863 most woodlands had been cleared for farmland or pasture.
14/9 | woodlots | The Round Tops were a significant woodland area in 1863; the west side of Little Round Top probably gave saw timber; wood removal on Big Round Top was prohibited by its owner | - | 1863 | hedged | The plan says that in 1863 Big and Little Round Tops constituted a significant woodland area near Gettysburg, that the west side of Little Round Top probably provided some saw timber for the town and local farms before the battle, and that removal of wood on Big Round Top was prohibited by its owner | =In 1863 Big and Little Round Tops constituted a significant woodland area in the vicinity of Gettysburg. The west side of Little Round Top probably provided some saw timber for the town and local farms before the battle; removal of wood on Big Round Top was prohibited by its owner.
14/9 | woodlots | Most historic farms had a woodlot; nine historic wooded areas on the southern part of the second-day battlefield | - | 1863-07 | stated | The plan says most historic farms had a woodlot or woods that provided almost all the needs for timber, firewood, furniture and tools, and that there were nine historic wooded areas on the southern part of the second-day battlefield | =Most historic farms had a woodlot or woods that provided almost all the needs for timber, firewood, furniture, and tools. There were nine historic wooded areas on the southern part of the second-day battlefield.
14/9 | orchards | The Sherfy peach orchard: the largest and most significant orchard of the battlefield, a pioneer orchard with a local canning industry, 13 acres originally | bld-sherfy-farm,FEN-sherfy-orchard | 1863-07 | stated | The plan says many historic farms included orchards, that the Sherfy peach orchard is the largest and most historically significant orchard identified on the battlefield, a pioneer orchard where new growing methods were used and the focal point of a small local canning industry, and that the original orchard was 13 acres | =Many of the historic farms included orchards. The largest and most historically significant orchard identified on the battlefield is the Sherfy peach orchard. This orchard was a pioneer orchard where new growing methods were used, and it was the focal point of a small, local canning industry. The original orchard was 13 acres;
14/9 | orchards | Other orchards of the south end: at the Rose, Bushman and Trostle farms | bld-rose-farm,bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The plan says other orchards in the south end of the park included those at the Rose, Bushman and Trostle farms | =Other orchards in the south end of the park included those at the Rose, Bushman, and Trostle farms.
14/9 | roads | The Emmitsburg and Wheatfield roads the only historic roads of the project area; historic lanes to the Bushman, Slyder and Rose farms | bld-slyder-farm,bld-rose-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The plan says the Emmitsburg and Wheatfield roads are the only historic roads in the project area and that the historic lanes include those to the Bushman, Slyder and Rose farms | =Emmitsburg and Wheatfield roads are the only historic roads in the project area; historic lanes include those to the Bushman, Slyder, and Rose farms.
14/9 | avenues | After the war avenues were built along the battle lines; 17 miles transferred to the federal government in 1895 | - | !1863-1895 (avenues built after the war by the Memorial Association) | stated | The plan says that after the war avenues were constructed to give access to important battle action areas and that in 1895 the 17 miles of park avenues built by the Memorial Association were transferred to the federal government | =After the war, avenues were constructed to provide access to important battle action areas. In 1895 the 17 miles of park avenues that had been built under the auspices of the Gettysburg Battlefield Memorial Association were transferred to the federal government.
9/4 | woodlots | Trees and brush removed from the west slope of Little Round Top and Houck's Ridge in the 1980s to re-create the historic scene | - | ~1980s (clearing before 1985; not the state of 1863) | stated | The plan says that over the last several years trees and brush have been removed from the west slope of Little Round Top and Houck's Ridge to re-create the historic scene and vistas | =Over the last several years, trees and brush have been removed from the west slope of Little Round Top and Houck's Ridge to re-create the historic scene and vistas.
18/13 | woodlots | Some existing woodlands cover land that was cleared at the time of the battle | - | ~1985 (condition of 1985 compared with the battle) | stated | The plan says some of the existing woodlands have taken over areas that were cleared lands at the time of the battle | =Some of the existing woodlands have taken over areas that were cleared lands at the time of the battle.
